      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The area was originally inhabited by the Timucua people, who occupied much of northern Florida before European contact in the 16th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lake_hart_florida",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "After the war, Orange County evolved from cattle ranching introduced by Spanish colonists and expanded by “Cracker” cowboys who drove herds across open prairies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lake_hart_florida",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Seasonal flooding keeps Lake Hart’s water level between about 55 and 64 feet, with a FEMA 100‑year flood elevation of 63 feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
